<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://wiki.ros.org/xacro">

  <!--
    The tool on the end of the arm: a Robotiq FT 300-S force/torque sensor on
    the flange, Robotiq's own UR coupling on the sensor, and a Robotiq 2F-85
    two-finger gripper on the coupling.

    Almost nothing about the gripper is described here, because Robotiq publish
    it. `robotiq_description` ships the links, the joint limits, the masses and
    the meshes for the 2F-85, and the coupling that bolts it to a UR flange, so
    this file includes that package and adds only what it does not cover: the
    force sensor, the friction of a silicone pad on glass, and a note about how
    the fingers are set up.

    Three things about this tool exist because it handles glass.

    **The fingers are pinned parallel.** The 2F-85 is an *adaptive* gripper:
    each finger has two joints driven by one motor, so left alone the fingers
    curl inwards and wrap around whatever they hold. That is useful for odd
    shapes and wrong for a glass, because this project's grip rules all assume
    two flat pads pressing on an upright piece of wall. Robotiq sell a parallel
    locking kit that pins the linkage so the pads stay parallel through the
    whole stroke, and the description below is of a gripper with that kit
    fitted — the fingertip joints mimic the knuckle at -1, which is exactly
    what keeps the pad faces level.

    **A force sensor sits between the flange and the gripper.** It is how the
    arm finds out what a glass really weighs, which no camera can tell it, and
    how it knows the rack has taken the weight before the fingers open. The
    UR5e has a force sensor built into its own flange, which would have been
    free, but Universal Robots publish +/-4.0 N of accuracy for it and a wine
    glass weighs about 2 N, so it cannot do this job. The FT 300-S resolves
    about 0.1 N.

    **The pads are silicone, so the friction is set here.** A rigid pad touches
    a curved glass wall at one point and all of the grip force goes through
    that point. A soft pad spreads over a small patch instead, which both holds
    better and is what makes a slightly sloping wall safe to grip: the pad
    conforms to the slope rather than sliding off it. The 6 degree figure in
    glasses/profile.py comes from how much slope a pad can take up. Robotiq's
    description says nothing about friction, because friction is not a property
    of a shape, so the surface below is this project's own.

    One thing worth knowing before reading the numbers elsewhere. The 2F-85 has
    **one driven joint**, a knuckle, and both fingers hang off it through a
    four-bar linkage. So the gripper cannot be told a width at all; it is told
    an angle, and the width follows. arm/robotiq_2f85.py does that translation
    and explains what else follows from it.

    Along the tool's z axis:
      0.0000  tool0, the flange
      0.0375  the force sensor ends, the coupling begins
      0.0485  the gripper's own base, where its description starts measuring
      0.1788  the middle of the pads with the gripper open (GRASP_DEPTH)
      0.1979  the fingertips (FINGERTIP_OFFSET)
  -->

  <xacro:include filename="$(find robotiq_description)/urdf/robotiq_2f_85_macro.urdf.xacro"/>
  <xacro:include filename="$(find robotiq_description)/urdf/ur_to_robotiq_adapter.urdf.xacro"/>

  <xacro:macro name="parallel_gripper" params="prefix parent">

    <!-- Robotiq publish 89 mm across on the robot side and 75 mm on the tool
         side, 37.5 mm thick and 442 g. It is drawn as the larger of the two
         diameters, because what this shape is really used for is working out
         how low the tool can reach before it touches the table, and there the
         widest part is the part that matters. -->
    <xacro:property name="sensor_height" value="0.0375"/>
    <xacro:property name="sensor_diameter" value="0.089"/>

    <!-- The sensor's own joint. It has to be a real joint rather than a weld,
         because that is where Gazebo reads the wrench, and it is why the
         gripper is not simply bolted to the flange. -->
    <joint name="${prefix}force_sensor_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${parent}"/>
      <child link="${prefix}force_sensor_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${prefix}force_sensor_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${sensor_height / 2}"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="${sensor_diameter / 2}" length="${sensor_height}"/></geometry>
        <material name="sensor_grey"><color rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/></material>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${sensor_height / 2}"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="${sensor_diameter / 2}" length="${sensor_height}"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${sensor_height / 2}"/>
        <mass value="0.442"/>
        <!-- A solid disc of this size and mass. -->
        <inertia ixx="0.00027" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="0.00027" iyz="0" izz="0.00044"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>

    <!-- Robotiq's own coupling, and then the gripper on top of it. The
         coupling's mount face is 11 mm above its own plate, which the adapter
         macro accounts for, so the gripper is attached to the mount link it
         provides rather than at an offset worked out here. -->
    <joint name="${prefix}sensor_to_coupling_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${prefix}force_sensor_link"/>
      <child link="${prefix}coupling_base_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${sensor_height}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>
    <link name="${prefix}coupling_base_link"/>

    <xacro:ur_to_robotiq prefix="${prefix}" connected_to="${prefix}coupling_base_link"/>

    <!-- ros2_control is declared in arm.urdf.xacro along with the arm's own
         joints and the Gazebo plugin that drives them, so the gripper must not
         declare a second one of its own. Switching it off here is also why no
         simulator is named: which simulator to generate a hardware interface
         for only matters inside the block that is not being generated. -->
    <xacro:robotiq_gripper
      name="RobotiqGripper"
      prefix="${prefix}"
      parent="${prefix}gripper_mount_link"
      include_ros2_control="false">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </xacro:robotiq_gripper>

    <!--
      A silicone pad on dry glass. This is the single number most worth
      checking against a real pad, because every grip force in
      glasses/force.py scales with it.

      It is set on the fingertips rather than on the whole finger because the
      fingertip is the only part that is meant to touch a glass. A glass
      touched by the knuckle has already been hit.
    -->
    <xacro:macro name="pad_surface" params="link">
      <gazebo reference="${link}">
        <mu1>0.6</mu1>
        <mu2>0.6</mu2>
        <kp>60000.0</kp>
        <kd>30.0</kd>
      </gazebo>
    </xacro:macro>

    <xacro:pad_surface link="${prefix}robotiq_85_left_finger_tip_link"/>
    <xacro:pad_surface link="${prefix}robotiq_85_right_finger_tip_link"/>

  </xacro:macro>

</robot>
